          "type": "turn",
          "question": "And what should I be eating?",
          "answer": "For general healthy eating, a dietary pattern that emphasizes vegetables, fruit, whole grains, low-fat dairy, poultry, fish, beans, nuts, and vegetable oils is often recommended. This approach suggests limiting saturated fat, full-fat dairy, fatty meat, sweets, and sugar-sweetened drinks. It's about the overall pattern of eating rather than focusing on a single food. (Source: General clinical reference and dietary pattern guidance)\n\nIf your body weight is above a healthy range, reducing it can improve glucose control, blood pressure, and lipid levels. Even a sustained reduction of five percent of body weight can provide measurable benefits. For some individuals, particularly earlier in the course of type 2 diabetes, significant weight loss can even lead to remission, meaning normal glucose levels without medication. (Source: National diabetes association nutrition guidance)\n\nWhen considering food choices, remember that \"sugar-free\" does not mean \"carbohydrate-free,\" as starches can raise glucose levels similar to sugar. Also, whole fruit is generally part of recommended eating patterns. (Source: National diabetes association nutrition guidance)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
